  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 122 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nSurface analysis indicates weak low pressure over northeast\nArkansas, with a boundary extending northeast toward Paducah, KY,\nand southwest into the ArkLaTex. Isolated showers and\nthunderstorms persist near this feature. Water vapor imagery\nshows broad zonal flow across the Southeast, anchored by a weak\nupper-level trough over the Southern Plains.\n\nModest height falls interacting with moderate instability (MLCAPE\n~2000 J/kg) and ~25 kt bulk shear will support multicell clusters\nthrough early evening, capable of producing 30-50 mph wind gusts.\nGust potential will diminish post-sunset as instability wanes and\nlow-level capping develops. Training convective cells remain a\nsecondary concern overnight as upper-level flow runs parallel to\nthe stalling frontal boundary near I-40, though confidence in\nlocalized flooding remains low.\n\nThe upper trough will slowly cross the Mid-South through Friday,\nlowering 500mb heights to ~586 dam. Debris cloudiness and a\npositively tilted trough axis will limit severe organization, but\nscattered diurnally enhanced convection (40-60% PoPs) is\nexpected. Increased cloud cover and convective outflow will hold\nhigh temperatures down, keeping heat indices in the mid-to-upper\n90s.\n\nExcessive heat returns Sunday through mid-week as a strong\n~594 dam ridge expands eastward. High confidence in LREF ensemble\nguidance points to building heat headlines and worsening drought\nconditions across the region.\n\nAC3",
